# Copy to dev/.env on the host that runs the container. .env is gitignored — it holds
# absolute host paths, and INFRA_SECRETS points at live key material.
#
# Absolute paths, never $HOME: HOME is unset or root's under sudo and cron, and the
# Makefile runs docker through sudo. A $HOME here works by hand and breaks the moment
# anything automates it. That rule is one of this repo's three, in ../README.md.

# The infra clone on this host. Bind-mounted at /workspace; the container's `git pull`
# and the host's are then the same pull, on one working tree.
INFRA_SRC=/home/gavin/infra

# Mode-700 directory OUTSIDE the repo, written by the dev-container playbook, holding
# exact names:
#   authorized_keys            public keys allowed to ssh INTO the container
#   tunnel.json                Cloudflare tunnel credentials — written by the SEPARATE
#                              cloudflare-dev-tunnel playbook, see below
#   id_ed25519_infra_deploy    GitHub deploy key for gsfernandes81/infra (generated on zero)
#   id_ed25519_fleet           the key that reaches zero, one and two   (generated on zero)
#   ssh_config.fleet           the three Host blocks, lifted from this host's ~/.ssh/config
#   known_hosts.fleet          their host keys, lifted from this host's ~/.ssh/known_hosts
# Mounted read-only; the entrypoint copies out of it and never writes back.
INFRA_SECRETS=/home/gavin/.infra-dev-secrets

# The in-container sshd, published on the host. 2222 is dd-dev, 2223 is ds-dev and
# 2224 is or3-dev on this same box, so this one is 2225.
DEV_SSH_PORT=2225

# The address that port is published on. Loopback by default — see compose.yaml. A
# cloudflared running on zero itself reaches it there. Set this to the docker bridge
# gateway (e.g. 172.17.0.1) only if cloudflared runs in a container of its own and so
# cannot see the host's loopback. Not 0.0.0.0: that publishes it to the home LAN too.
DEV_SSH_BIND=127.0.0.1

# The container's hostname, and the machine title Claude Code shows. Worth keeping
# distinct from the other three on this box — they all look alike once you are inside.
DEV_HOSTNAME=infra-dev

# cgroup v2 memory.max / memory.swap.max for this container alone. Lower than
# or3-dev's 1536m on purpose: that container builds Python C extensions, this one runs
# ansible and a claude. `make status` prints oom=true if this turns out to be wrong.
DEV_MEM_LIMIT=1024m
DEV_MEMSWAP_LIMIT=1536m

# Commit identity inside the container.
GIT_USER_NAME=Gavin Fernandes
GIT_USER_EMAIL=gavinfernandes2012@pm.me

# ── Cloudflare ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The tunnel's public hostname. EMPTY BY DEFAULT, and empty means no tunnel: the
# container comes up and is reached over DEV_SSH_PORT above, exactly as if none of the
# Cloudflare machinery existed. That is what makes the tunnel additive rather than a
# thing the container now depends on to be usable.
#
# Before setting it, provision the Cloudflare side — from the ansible directory, not
# from here, because it is management-plane work and not container config:
#
#     cd ~/infra/ansible
#     ansible-playbook playbooks/cloudflare-dev-tunnel.yml --check   # prove first
#     ansible-playbook playbooks/cloudflare-dev-tunnel.yml
#
# That writes tunnel.json into INFRA_SECRETS above and prints the phone's service-token
# credentials once. Then set this and `make up` — NOT `make restart`, which does not
# re-read compose.yaml.
DEV_TUNNEL_HOSTNAME=
